# Two Sum

## Problem

Given an array of integers and a target, find two numbers whose sum equals the target.

---

## 1. Brute Force

Check every possible pair.

**Time:** O(n²)
**Space:** O(1)

---

## 2. HashMap

Store previously seen numbers and check whether the required complement exists.

**Time:** O(n)
**Space:** O(n)

This is the standard optimal solution for the usual unsorted-array Two Sum problem.

---

## Edge Cases

* Duplicate numbers
* Negative numbers
* Target = 0
* No valid pair
* Array with only two elements

Brute Force — O(n²)
import java.util.*;

class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);

        int n = sc.nextInt();
        int[] arr = new int[n];

        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            arr[i] = sc.nextInt();
        }

        int target = sc.nextInt();

        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            for (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {

                if (arr[i] + arr[j] == target) {
                    System.out.println(i + " " + j);
                    return;
                }
            }
        }

        System.out.println("-1 -1");
    }
}

Optimal — HashMap O(n)
import java.util.*;

class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);

        int n = sc.nextInt();
        int[] arr = new int[n];

        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            arr[i] = sc.nextInt();
        }

        int target = sc.nextInt();

        HashMap<Integer, Integer> map = new HashMap<>();

        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {

            int needed = target - arr[i];

            if (map.containsKey(needed)) {
                System.out.println(map.get(needed) + " " + i);
                return;
            }

            map.put(arr[i], i);
        }

        System.out.println("-1 -1");
    }
}
